{"sections":{"image_banner":{"type":"image-banner","blocks":{"heading":{"type":"heading","settings":{"heading":"Authentic Luxury Shokupan","heading_size":"h0"}},"text":{"type":"text","settings":{"text":"A Japanese elevated take on a Canadian staple","text_style":"body"}},"button":{"type":"buttons","settings":{"button_label_1":"Order","button_link_1":"shopify:\/\/products\/shokupan","button_style_secondary_1":true,"button_label_2":"","button_link_2":"shopify:\/\/blogs\/recipes","button_style_secondary_2":false}}},"block_order":["heading","text","button"],"disabled":true,"settings":{"image":"shopify:\/\/shop_images\/IMG_0027.jpg","image_overlay_opacity":40,"image_height":"medium","image_behavior":"none","desktop_content_position":"bottom-center","desktop_content_alignment":"center","show_text_box":false,"color_scheme":"background-1","stack_images_on_mobile":false,"mobile_content_alignment":"center","show_text_below":false}},"rich_text":{"type":"rich-text","blocks":{"heading":{"type":"heading","settings":{"heading":"Ottawa's Japanese bread bakery","heading_size":"h1"}},"text":{"type":"text","settings":{"text":"<p>Welcome. Browse our current bake below — a living menu of shokupan and seasonal loaves, with photos and prices. We don’t take orders online; this site is here so you can see what we make.<\/p>"}}},"block_order":["heading","text"],"settings":{"desktop_content_position":"center","content_alignment":"center","color_scheme":"background-1","full_width":true,"padding_top":40,"padding_bottom":0}},"16534287435f3abd5f":{"type":"rich-text","blocks":{"1653428743d89a5e36-0":{"type":"heading","settings":{"heading":"What is Shokupan?","heading_size":"h2"}},"1653428743d89a5e36-1":{"type":"text","settings":{"text":"<p>Japan is generally regarded as being a rice-based food culture. However, bread — or <em>pan <\/em>in Japanese, derived from the Portuguese word <em>pão <\/em>— is eaten almost as widely. Many people prefer toast for breakfast over a bowl of rice. Sandwiches and savory breads called <em>kashipan<\/em> are just as popular for lunch as <em>bentō<\/em> and <em>onigiri<\/em> rice balls.<\/p><p>The most ubiquitous type of bread in Japan is the white and pillowy square-shaped bread called <em>shokupan<\/em>, which simply means “eating bread.” Made of white flour, yeast, milk or milk powder, butter, salt and sugar, shokupan is both loved and taken for granted by most.<\/p><p><\/p><p>Our bread is a special kind of Shokupan similar to those found in Osaka's famous <a href=\"https:\/\/nogaminopan.com\/en\/\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"https:\/\/nogaminopan.com\/en\/\">Nogami Bakery<\/a> and is an excellent toasting bread. Check out some of our favourite recipes!<\/p>"}},"1653428743d89a5e36-2":{"type":"button","settings":{"button_label":"Recipes","button_link":"shopify:\/\/blogs\/recipes","button_style_secondary":false,"button_label_2":"","button_link_2":"","button_style_secondary_2":false}}},"block_order":["1653428743d89a5e36-0","1653428743d89a5e36-1","1653428743d89a5e36-2"],"settings":{"desktop_content_position":"center","content_alignment":"center","color_scheme":"background-1","full_width":true,"padding_top":24,"padding_bottom":48}},"featured_collection":{"type":"featured-collection","settings":{"collection":"all","products_to_show":25,"title":"Menu","heading_size":"h1","description":"<p>Photos and prices for what we bake. Open an item to read more about it.<\/p>","show_description":false,"description_style":"body","columns_desktop":3,"enable_desktop_slider":false,"full_width":false,"show_view_all":false,"view_all_style":"solid","color_scheme":"background-1","image_ratio":"square","image_shape":"default","show_secondary_image":false,"show_vendor":false,"show_rating":false,"quick_add":"none","columns_mobile":"2","swipe_on_mobile":false,"padding_top":36,"padding_bottom":48}},"collage":{"type":"collage","blocks":{"image_JCmCfr":{"type":"image","settings":{"image":"shopify:\/\/shop_images\/9A506239-3ABB-4D38-A5AA-8A3F9F600B3A_1_201_a.j